If you do:

```cpp
IncreaseDateByXDays(30 * XMonths, Date)
```

it will be **wrong** whenever a month isn’t 30 days (which is… most of the time). Months have **28/29/30/31** days, so “1 month later” is *not* the same as “30 days later”.

### Correct approach: add months as months
When you add months, you normally:
1. Increase the **month/year** by `XMonths`
2. Keep the **day** if possible
3. If the target month doesn’t have that day, **clamp** to the last day of the target month  
   (e.g., `31/01 + 1 month` → `28/02` or `29/02` in leap years)

Below is a clean C++14 way to do that using your existing `NumberOfDaysInAMonth`.

<llm-snippet-file>main.cpp</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
#include <iostream>

using namespace std;

// ... existing code ...

short NumberOfDaysInAMonth(short Month, short Year) {
    if (Month < 1 || Month > 12) return 0;

    short days[12] = { 31, 28, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31, 31, 30, 31, 30, 31 };
    return (Month == 2) ? (isLeapYear(Year) ? 29 : 28) : days[Month - 1];
}

// Add this helper:
short ClampDayToMonth(short Day, short Month, short Year)
{
    short lastDay = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Month, Year);
    if (Day < 1) return 1;
    if (Day > lastDay) return lastDay;
    return Day;
}

// Add this function:
sDate IncreaseDateByXMonths(short XMonths, sDate Date)
{
    int totalMonths = (Date.Year * 12) + (Date.Month - 1) + XMonths; // 0-based month index
    Date.Year = totalMonths / 12;
    Date.Month = (totalMonths % 12) + 1;

    Date.Day = ClampDayToMonth(Date.Day, Date.Month, Date.Year);
    return Date;
}

// ... existing code ...
```

### Why this solves your question
- Adding months uses the *calendar month length* automatically.
- No guessing with 30-day months.
- Handles February + leap years correctly.

If you want, I can also add `DecreaseDateByXMonths` and clarify what to do for tricky cases like `29/02` when moving to a non-leap year.

