Yes and no—your versions are **conceptually fine**, but the **“faster”** one is **not correct for all dates** unless you add one extra step.

## 1) Your loop version (safe, but slower)
```cpp
stDate IncreaseDateByXYears(short Years, stDate Date)
{
    for (short i = 1; i <= Years; i++)
    {
        Date = IncreaseDateByOneYear(Date);
    }
    return Date;
}
```

- **Correctness:** ✅ *Only if* `IncreaseDateByOneYear()` is correct (it must handle 29 Feb / last-day-of-month cases).
- **Performance:** ⚠️ O(Years). For `Years = 10` it’s trivial, but for large values it’s unnecessary work.

So this is “better” than a naive fast method, because it’s robust (again: depends on `IncreaseDateByOneYear`).

## 2) Your “faster” version (fast, but can produce invalid dates)
```cpp
stDate IncreaseDateByXYearsFaster(short Years, stDate Date)
{
    Date.Year += Years;
    return Date;
}
```

- **Performance:** ✅ O(1)
- **Correctness:** ❌ can create impossible dates.

Example:
- Input: `29/2/2020`
- Add 1 year → `29/2/2021` (invalid; 2021 isn’t leap)

Also dates like `31/1` are fine, but anything involving Feb 29 is the classic failure.

## Best practical approach
Use the O(1) method **plus clamping** (validate the day in the target month/year):

```cpp
stDate IncreaseDateByXYearsFaster(short Years, stDate Date)
{
    Date.Year += Years;
    short last = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > last) Date.Day = last;
    return Date;
}
```

That gives you:
- **Fast:** O(1)
- **Correct:** ✅ for “same month/day if possible, otherwise last valid day of that month”

## Summary
- Your **loop version** is fine (safe) but slower.
- Your **fast version** is **not safe** unless you clamp/adjust the day.
- The **best** is the fast version **with** the “last day in target month” check.

