Your simplified versions will **work for most dates**, but they’re **wrong for some important cases**—specifically when the current date would become **invalid** after changing the year.

### Why the clamping is needed
Example: `29/02/2024` (leap day)

- If you do only `Year--`, you get `29/02/2023`  
  But **2023 isn’t a leap year**, so `29/02/2023` is **not a valid date**.

That’s why your current implementation recalculates the days in the month and clamps:

- `29/02/2024 - 1 year` should become `28/02/2023`

So: **don’t remove the “adjust Day if it exceeds days in month” part**.

### Best approach (keep logic, reduce duplication)
You *can* simplify while staying correct by making the “faster” version the single source of truth, and let the others call it.

<llm-snippet-file>Project.cpp</llm-snippet-file>
```cpp
sDate DecreaseDateByOneYear(sDate Date)
{
    return DecreaseDateByXYearsFaster(1, Date);
}

sDate DecreaseDateByXYears(short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    return DecreaseDateByXYearsFaster(XYears, Date);
}

sDate DecreaseDateByXYearsFaster(short XYears, sDate Date)
{
    Date.Year -= XYears;

    short NumberOfDaysInCurrentMonth = NumberOfDaysInAMonth(Date.Month, Date.Year);
    if (Date.Day > NumberOfDaysInCurrentMonth)
    {
        Date.Day = NumberOfDaysInCurrentMonth;
    }

    return Date;
}
```

This keeps the correctness (handles Feb 29, etc.) and also avoids repeating the “clamp” logic in multiple places.

If you *really* want the loop-based `DecreaseDateByXYears` for learning purposes, keep it—but still keep the clamp inside `DecreaseDateByOneYear` (or inside the faster one, and call it).

