If you mean **`this`** in C++, you use it to refer to the **current object** inside a class member function.

## Why it exists

When a method runs, it needs a way to know **which object** it is working on.

For example, if you have:

```cpp
class A {
public:
    int x;
    void SetX(int x) {
        this->x = x;
    }
};
```

- `x` on the right side is the **parameter**
- `this->x` is the **member variable of the current object**

Without `this`, the names would conflict.

## In your case

If a parameter and a member have the same name, `this` helps distinguish them clearly.

## When you need it

You usually use `this` when:

- a parameter has the same name as a member variable
- you want to return the current object
- you want to pass the current object to something else
- you want to make code clearer about which variable you mean

## When you don’t need it

If names are different, you often don’t need `this` at all:

```cpp
class A {
private:
    int value;
public:
    void SetValue(int newValue) {
        value = newValue;
    }
};
```

Here, `this` is optional.

## Short version

Use `this` when you need to say:

> “I mean the object’s member, not the local variable.”

If you want, I can show this using your `FirstName` example in a very simple way.

